  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Thu Jun 25 2026\n\nA slow-moving frontal zone and deep plume of Gulf moisture remain \ndraped across the central Plains, placing northeast Kansas on the \nnorthern edge of a broader heavy rain and thunderstorm corridor \nextending from southern Kansas into southwest Missouri. Precipitable \nwater values near the climatological max for this time of the year \nand model QPF support efficient rainfall processes. WPC\u2019s Day 1 \nExcessive Rainfall Outlook highlights a Moderate Risk over southeast \nKansas with a broader Slight Risk extending north into portions of \neastern Kansas, consistent with widespread 2-4 inch totals and \nlocalized higher amounts where convection trains. Therefore, the\nFlood Watch continues through early Friday morning. \n\nThrough this afternoon and evening, periods of showers and \nthunderstorms will continue, with the heaviest rain most likely this \nevening into tonight as the nocturnal low-level jet strengthens and \nfocuses convergence along and north of the boundary. High-resolution \nguidance favors a corridor of heavier QPF southwest and south of the \nCWA, but embedded heavier cells and short training segments are still\nplausible across eastern and central Kansas, especially near and \nsouth of I 70, where localized flash flooding could develop in low- \nlying and poor-drainage areas. Confidence is moderate to high in \ncontinued heavy rain, but lower in the precise placement of maximum \namounts given sensitivity to convective evolution and outflow \ninteractions. The Flood Watch was expanded slightly north as several \nmodels over several runs were hinting at a narrow heavier band of \nrain developing late tonight north of I-70. \n\nRegarding severe potential, guidance keeps the more organized severe\nthreat focused across southern Kansas into northern Oklahoma and the\nOzarks, where a corridor of stronger instability, deep-layer shear, \nand an intensifying low-level jet are focused. Our area lies on the \nnorthern fringe of this environment; widespread stratiform rain and \nlimited surface-based instability should largely temper severe \ncoverage, but a few stronger cores this afternoon and evening could \nbriefly produce marginally severe wind gusts or small hail where \nmesoscale pockets of instability persist. \n\nBy Friday morning, the main heavy rain axis and strongest forcing \nshift east, with some lighter showers potentially lingering into the \nlate morning across far eastern Kansas. Behind today\u2019s system, mid- \nlevel ridging and warm advection build into the Plains, with highs \nrebounding into the upper 80s Saturday and low 90s by Sunday under \nincreasing sunshine, while overnight lows climb into the mid to upper\n70s. This is near to slightly above late-June climatological normals\n(average June highs around the upper 80s and lows in the low 70s). \n\nInto early next week, ensemble and medium-range guidance favor even \nmore hot and humid conditions developing across the central U.S., \nwith highs generally in the mid 90s each day and dewpoints in the \nupper 60s to low 70s, yielding heat indices commonly in the upper 90s\nto low 100s. A typical summer time diurnal thunderstorm pattern may \naccompany this heat but confidence in timing and coverage is low and \nand the primary threat over the next week shifts from flooding to \nheat stress for outdoor activities and vulnerable populations.",
